al diameter is 15/20/25 — 3 values. Connection threads: G3/4\" / G1 / G1¼\" — 3 values. Length: 110–165 / 130–195 / 190–225 — 3 values (ranges per diameter).\n\nPressure loss class: ΔP40 (five columns), ΔP63 (last column) — 6 columns.\n\nSo there are 6 \"columns\" — but 3 nominal diameters. Actually likely it's a table with 6 columns: perhaps DN15 has Q3=1.6 (R=800, 400, 250 variants), DN20 has Q3=2.5 (R=800, 400, 250), DN20 with Q3=4.0 (R=1600, 800, 400, 250)... Hmm.\n\nThe Q3/Q1 row: \"800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250 (per column)\". This suggests each column can have multiple ratio variants: columns 1-3 (DN15?): 800, 400, 250 and columns 4-6: 1600, 800, 400, 250? That's confusing. \n\nGiven the fragment doesn't include the actual table (pages 5-9 presumably have \"Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" table), I should be careful. The Q3/Q1 information is ambiguous. I could record it as a matrix table with the raw values, or describe it.\n\nSince this is a family of models, I should populate model_family.models. The models vary by nominal diameter and Q3. Given the data: Q3 = 1.6 / 2.5 / 2.5 / 4.0 / 4.0 / 6.3 (6 variants). Nominal diameter 15/20/25 (3 values). Likely mapping: DN15 → Q3 1.6; DN20 → Q3 2.5 (×2 columns?); DN25 → Q3 4.0, 4.0, 6.3? Hmm.\n\nActually a common WMP-type table: 6 columns:\n- DN15, Q3=1.6, R=800/400/250\n- DN20, Q3=2.5, R=800/400/250\n- DN25, Q3=2.5? No...\n\nHmm, \"Q₃/Q₁: 800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250 (per column)\". Maybe it means: for some columns the ratio options are 800, 400, 250 and for others 1600, 800, 400, 250. The slash \"/\" might separate groups of columns. If 6 columns: first 3 columns (Q3=1.6, 2.5, 2.5): R ∈ {800, 400, 250}; last 3 columns (Q3=4.0, 4.0, 6.3): R ∈ {1600, 800, 400, 250}. Hmm but that's odd too.\n\nAlternatively first group: {800, 400, 250} applies to columns 1-3 and {1600, 800, 400, 250} applies to columns 4-6.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I shouldn't fabricate exact per-model mapping. But I can capture what's present. Let me model the model family with 6 variants based on Q3 (since that's the clearest per-column data), and note nominal diameter per column is uncertain... Actually wait — 6 Q3 values and 3 DN values. The likely correspondence given typical water meters:\n\nDN15: Q3 1.6\nDN20: Q3 2.5\nDN25: Q3 2.5? Unusual. Typically DN25 → Q3 4.0 or 6.3.\n\nPossible: DN15 → 1.6; DN20 → 2.5, 2.5 (two variants?); DN25 → 4.0, 4.0, 6.3? Hmm.\n\nConnection threads: G3/4\" (DN15), G1 (DN20), G1¼\" (DN25). Lengths: 110–165 (DN15), 130–195 (DN20), 190–225 (DN25).\n\nPressure loss: ΔP40 for five columns, ΔP63 for last column. Last column Q3=6.3 → ΔP63.\n\nGiven uncertainty about exact DN-to-column mapping, I should avoid fabricating. The safest approach: create model_family with models identified by column, using Q3 values as identifiers where DN mapping is unclear, OR use combined identifiers like \"DN15, Q3=1.6\" only where confident.\n\nHmm. Actually, let me reconsider. The nominal diameters listed: 15 / 20 / 25. Q3: 1.6 / 2.5 / 2.5 / 4.0 / 4.0 / 6.3. If we assume the sequence maps progressively: columns 1-2 → DN15 (1.6, 2.5)? No...\n\nActually maybe: DN15: Q3=1.6 and 2.5? Hmm, but that doesn't work either since there are 6 columns.\n\nCommon pattern for such meters (e.g., ADD Production WMP): The table likely has 6 columns:\n1. DN15, Q3=1.6\n2. DN20, Q3=2.5\n3. DN20, Q3=4.0\n4. DN25, Q3=4.0\n5. DN25, Q3=6.3\n...\n\nBut we have Q3 = 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4.0, 4.0, 6.3 — two 2.5s and two 4.0s. So:\n1. DN15, Q3=1.6\n2. DN20, Q3=2.5\n3. DN25, Q3=2.5?? unusual but possible for R=1600? No wait, DN25 with Q3=2.5 would give very low R with Q1 = 2.5/800... hmm.\n\nActually maybe: \n1. DN15 Q3=1.6\n2. DN15 Q3=2.5? No.\n\nI really can't determine this. The instructions say \"Don't fabricate.\" So I'll define models by Q3 value primarily, and include nominal_diameter as a model_level characteristic with the set of values 15/20/25 — but per-model mapping unknown.\n\nAlternative: I could put nominal diameter as a type_level characteristic with a list... but rules say lists of values indicate a model matrix. Since the mapping between the 3 DN values and 6 columns is not given, I could either:\n- Emit model_level entry with values only where identifiable (can't).\n- Emit nominal_diameter as model_level with 6 values each unknown — no.\n\nBetter: keep model_family.models with model_ids based on Q3: \"Q3=1.6 m³/h\", etc. And emit model_level characteristics:\n- q3: 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4.0, 4.0, 6.3 m³/h\n- q3_q1_ratio: ambiguous — \"800; 400; 250\" for first group, \"1600; 800; 400; 250\" for second. Since \"(per column)\" it means each column has multiple possible ratios (meter variants with different R). Actually this means each Q3 column supports multiple ratio versions! So R (Q3/Q1) is a selectable variant within each column. So the models could be even more numerous.\n\nGiven this complexity and the fragment doesn't include the actual flowrate table (page says \"flowrates are shown in Table Basic metrological data (flowrates)\" — likely on pages 5+), the safest is:\n\n- model_family.family_name: \"WMP\"\n- models: 6 entries based on Q3 columns: e.g., \"WMP DN15 Q3=1.6\"... but DN mapping unknown.\n\nHmm, let me think about what's most defensible. The Q3 list gives 6 values for 6 columns. The DN list gives 3 values. Threads give 3 values. Lengths give 3 ranges. Most likely mapping (based on standard sizes):\n- G3/4\", 110–165mm → DN15\n- G1, 130–195mm → DN20\n- G1¼\", 190–225mm → DN25\n\nAnd Q3 mapping — with 6 columns and 3 DN sizes, each DN likely has 2 columns:\n- DN15: Q3 = 1.6, 2.5\n- DN20: Q3 = 2.5, 4.0\n- DN25: Q3 = 4.0, 6.3\n\nThat gives Q3 sequence: 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4.0, 4.0, 6.3 ✓ exactly matches! \n\nSo models:\n1. DN15, Q3=1.6, G3/4\", length 110–165, ΔP40\n2. DN15, Q3=2.5, G3/4\", length 110–165, ΔP40\n3. DN20, Q3=2.5, G1, length 130–195, ΔP40\n4. DN20, Q3=4.0, G1, length 130–195, ΔP40\n5. DN25, Q3=4.0, G1¼\", length 190–225, ΔP40\n6. DN25, Q3=6.3, G1¼\", length 190–225, ΔP63\n\nThis mapping is consistent: Q3/Q1 ratios: first group (cols 1-2? or 1-3?) \"800; 400; 250\" and second \"1600; 800; 400; 250\". Hmm, if first group is 3 columns... 6 columns split as 3+3: cols 1-3 (Q3=1.6, 2.5, 2.5): R ∈ {800, 400, 250}; cols 4-6 (Q3=4.0, 4.0, 6.3): R ∈ {1600, 800, 400, 250}. That's plausible too — smaller meters have lower max R.\n\nBut wait, \"800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250 (per column)\" — the slash could separate two rows of a wrapped table. With 6 columns: if the row is 800; 400; 250 for cols 1-3 and 1600; 800; 400; 250 for cols 4-6 — but that's 3 values then 4 values = 7 for 6 columns. Doesn't fit. If it's a wrapped line: first line \"800; 400; 250 /\" (wait the \"/\" is in there).\n\nHmm: \"800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250 (per column)\". Could be OCR of a 2-row cell: row1: 800; 400; 250 and row2: 1600; 800; 400; 250. If the column layout is 3 columns per... no, 6 columns.\n\nAlternative interpretation: each column cell contains multiple ratio options listed vertically. The transcription merged them. E.g., column A: 800, 400, 250 (3 options); columns B-F: 1600, 800, 400, 250 (4 options each)? Or column A: 800; column B: 400; ... no.\n\nActually with \"(per column)\" annotation, likely: for each column, the available Q3/Q1 ratios are: [800, 400, 250] for first set and [1600, 800, 400, 250] for second set. Given the slash, maybe first 3 columns have {800,400,250} and last 3 have {1600,800,400,250}.\n\nGiven genuine ambiguity, I'll capture this as a matrix_tables entry preserving the raw data, and include model_level for q3_q1_ratio with values as sets (strings like \"800, 400, 250\")? The rules say don't fabricate. I could record q3_q1_ratio per model group with value as string \"800; 400; 250\" — but values should be numbers when numeric... Lists of values should be model matrix. But here the ratio is genuinely multi-valued per column (variant options).\n\nApproach: I'll define models by column index with the 