00, 800, 400, 250\n\nHmm wait — but if the split is 3+3, that matches \"800; 400; 250\" (3 values) then \"1600; 800; 400; 250\" (4 values). But 3+4 = 7 values for 6 columns... The \"(per column)\" suggests each column has a set. If the first 3 columns each have {800,400,250} and last 3 each have {1600,800,400,250}, the transcription \"800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250 (per column)\" would represent: group1 ratios / group2 ratios. Hmm, but then why would the \"/\" be between? Possibly the original table row has two lines: line 1 under cols 1-3: \"800; 400; 250\", line 2 under cols 4-6: \"1600; 800; 400; 250\". That fits!\n\nActually another possibility: the row reads \"800; 400; 250 / 1600; 800; 400; 250\" where the first line applies to columns 1-2 (DN15: 1.6 and 2.5) and... no.\n\nI'll go with: columns with Q3 ≤ 2.5 → ratios {800, 400, 250}; columns with Q3 ≥ 4.0 → ratios {1600, 800, 400, 250}. That's my best inference. I'll record q3_q1_ratio as model_level with value as string list per model, e.g., \"800; 400; 250\". Or as a matrix_table. Given uncertainty, maybe I record it as a characteristic with string values documenting the options. The schema's model_level values[].value can be a string. I'll use value: \"800, 400, 250\" (options). Hmm, that could be misread as a single ratio. Alternatively \"800/400/250\". I'll add a footnote-ish description... There's no footnote. I'll put it in matrix_tables description.\n\nActually, let me include a matrix_table \"Metrological specifications\" capturing all the per-column data as transcribed, with description noting the column groupings, and model_level entries for the clear attributes (q3, pressure_loss_class, q3_q1_ratio options).\n\nNow other characteristics:\n\nType-level (whole cert):\n- measuring_principle: ultrasonic\n- accuracy_class: 2\n- temperature_class: T30 or T50 → this is a config/variant. Value: \"T30 or T50\". Hmm. Could record as type_level with value string \"T30 or T50\". Temperature class T30 mean